traceability.statement:
Reconstruct a real Indian hospital cashless pre-authorisation workflow from authoritative evidence.

Apollo Hospitals is used here as the **reference implementation**, not yet as the assumed ART customer.

The purpose is to understand:

- the real workflow;
- actors and ownership;
- inputs and evidence;
- decisions;
- exception states;
- existing capability;
- workflow requirements that can later be tested across mid-scale Indian hospitals.

No pain point, ART gap, ROI, or customer opportunity is assumed at this checkpoint.

---

Apollo publicly documents a detailed cashless hospitalisation process.

The evidence identifies:

- planned and emergency hospitalization;
- Corporate Help Desk involvement;
- insurance and identity documentation;
- TPA-specific pre-authorisation forms;
- doctor-completed treatment information;
- completeness checking;
- submission to the TPA;
- payer approval/rejection;
- enhancement when expenses exceed the approved amount.

**Evidence:** Apollo Hospitals official insurance documentation.  
https://www.apollohospitals.com/region/kolkata/insurance/

Apollo therefore provides a sufficiently visible workflow for reconstruction.

This does **not** mean every Indian hospital follows the same process or has the same capability maturity.

---

- health insurance card;
- government-issued ID;
- employee ID where applicable for corporate insurance.

- treatment information;
- treating doctor's completed and signed section;
- relevant supporting information.

The following remain **UNKNOWN**:

- workflow volume;
- exception frequency;
- query frequency;
- enhancement frequency;
- human handling time;
- employee workload;
- rework;
- SLA performance;
- internal software architecture;
- automation coverage;
- cross-system work;
- financial cost of exceptions;
- customer dissatisfaction attributable to the workflow;
- ART capability gap.

These must not be inferred from workflow complexity.

---

Apollo should therefore be treated as:

**REFERENCE / BENCHMARK**

not:

**ASSUMED CUSTOMER**

The future opportunity search can use the reconstructed workflow to identify other Indian hospitals where:

- the workflow exists;
- implementation maturity is lower;
- the same exception/problem is materially painful;
- an identifiable owner exists;
- existing systems do not adequately resolve it;
- ART can provide a differentiated capability.

No such customer is declared by OPP-001-01.

---

Reconstruct what capabilities already exist around the Apollo cashless pre-authorisation workflow before treating any part of the workflow as an ART opportunity.

Apollo remains the **reference implementation**.

The objective is not to prove that Apollo needs ART.

The objective is to establish:

- what the hospital already does;
- what external payer/TPA capability already exists;
- what Apollo's technology ecosystem already provides;
- which parts of the workflow are demonstrably automated;
- which parts remain unknown;
- where the reference implementation leaves an evidence boundary for further industry research.

---

Apollo's official insurance documentation establishes a defined Corporate / Insurance Help Desk process.

The documented capabilities include:

- collecting insurance and identity information;
- obtaining the relevant TPA pre-authorisation form;
- assisting with policy/general information;
- obtaining treatment information from the treating doctor;
- checking the completed form for completeness and discrepancies;
- sending the completed request to the TPA;
- communicating the approval status to the patient.

**Source:** Apollo Hospitals — Insurance / Cashless Hospitalization  
https://www.apollohospitals.com/region/kolkata/insurance/

This establishes an operational workflow with role ownership and control points.

It does **not** establish the internal software architecture supporting those activities.

---

A critical evidence point emerged during the investigation.

**Apollo HealthAxis**, an Apollo healthcare technology organization, publicly describes an **Ops Copilot** for front-desk and back-office operations.

Its official IT Services page states that the capability supports administrative, quality and support teams, including the **Insurance Desk**, and describes specialized AI agents for tasks including **insurance pre-authorisation**.

The same page describes capabilities including:

- AI-native applications;
- workflow-driven applications;
- AI assistants;
- autonomous agents;
- EHR integrations;
- data engineering;
- AI model development.

**Source:** Apollo HealthAxis — IT Services  
https://www.apollohealthaxis.com/capabilities/it-service

This is a major capability finding.

---

The evidence supports:

> Apollo's broader technology ecosystem has publicly described AI-agent capability targeting insurance pre-authorisation.

It does **not** support:

> Every Apollo hospital has deployed this capability.

It also does not establish:

- deployment coverage;
- adoption rate;
- automation percentage;
- exact workflow tasks automated;
- agent accuracy;
- exception coverage;
- actual productivity improvement;
- actual financial ROI;
- whether difficult cases still require manual investigation.

Therefore the correct classification is:

**EXISTING CAPABILITY: VERIFIED**

**ACTUAL DEPLOYMENT / COVERAGE: UNKNOWN**

---

Reconstruct what happens when the normal cashless pre-authorisation path does not resolve immediately, with specific focus on:

- query / clarification;
- insufficient information;
- enhancement;
- rejection;
- emergency processing;
- evidence collection;
- human ownership;
- existing AI capability.

The objective is to identify the **exception workflow**, not to assume that exceptions are automatically painful or commercially valuable.

Apollo remains the **reference workflow / capability benchmark**, not an assumed customer.

---

The source does not establish:

- who retrieves the additional information in every case;
- how many systems are searched;
- how long retrieval takes;
- how many query cycles occur;
- whether query responses are automated;
- cost of query handling.

These remain **UNKNOWN**.

---

- enhancement frequency;
- average enhancement amount;
- preparation time;
- documentation workload;
- number of follow-ups;
- rejection frequency;
- financial impact on Apollo.

---

The National Health Authority's PM-JAY documentation independently confirms that pre-authorisation can require:

- details from the treating doctor;
- a request for authorization;
- online submission through a scheme portal;
- medical-team interaction with the treating doctor where necessary;
- clarification for delay;
- communication of the pre-authorisation decision to the healthcare provider.

The NHA documentation also states that required documents are sent to the insurer/authority and that document requirements can vary by package.

**Sources:**

https://nha.gov.in/img/pmjay-files/RFE_Volume_II.pdf

https://nha.gov.in/img/resources/HBP-2.2-manual.pdf

This is useful as **independent Indian workflow evidence**, but it must not be treated as evidence of Apollo's internal process.

---

Apollo HealthAxis' public evidence proves that the Apollo technology ecosystem has AI capability targeting insurance pre-authorisation.

It does **not** prove:

- deployment at every Apollo hospital;
- adoption percentage;
- which exception paths are automated;
- which query types are automated;
- whether evidence retrieval is automated;
- whether difficult cases still require human investigation;
- actual productivity;
- actual ROI.

Therefore:

**AI CAPABILITY:** VERIFIED

**ACTUAL DEPLOYMENT / COVERAGE:** UNKNOWN

**RESIDUAL HUMAN BURDEN:** UNKNOWN

---

Apollo's documented workflow assigns human roles to:

- collect/complete information;
- check completeness;
- identify discrepancies;
- submit requests;
- communicate status;
- apply for enhancements;
- provide enhancement documentation.

We cannot yet prove that Apollo staff:

- manually search multiple systems;
- manually reconcile conflicting records;
- manually draft query responses;
- repeatedly chase TPAs;
- manually track every pending case;
- spend a material amount of time on exceptions;
- experience significant operational pain.

This distinction is critical.

> **Human involvement is proven. Material human investigation burden is not.**

---
